From: Jonathan Avis <jonathanavis96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:34:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Add Claude (Haiku/Sonnet) as a pluggable decision-maker
 and benchmark it against Jev

New jev_ultrafast/decision_claude.py implements DECISION_PROVIDER=claude-haiku|claude-sonnet
as a standing `claude -p` child asked for the same operation+target decision Jev makes, given
the identical element table, validated against the operations/targets actually on offer.
model.py:decide() dispatches on DECISION_PROVIDER (default jev, unchanged); agent.py now calls
decide() instead of choose() directly, the only change to the existing loop.

bench/run_bench.py + bench/run_one.py run the 27-trial sweep (3 goals x 3 arms x 3 reps, one
Chromium, strictly sequential, arms interleaved, each run in its own process group with a 120s
budget) and record bench/results-<timestamp>.jsonl. Arm M (Claude Code driving Playwright MCP
directly) was checked and skipped: no Playwright MCP server is registered for this account.

Findings appended to SPIKE-NOTES.md: Jev is faster and more reliable on every goal; Sonnet
matches Jev's accuracy but not its latency; Haiku degrades sharply as the element table/step
count grows (0/3 on the TYPE_TEXT goal, 1/3 on the multi-step goal). n=3 per cell, not
statistically significant, but the pattern held across all three goals.

Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
